# EN02.zip

Dataset: avia-gravd — NOAA NGS GRAV-D airborne gravity, block EN02 (main data lines)
File: data/avia/gravd/EN02.zip
SHA-256: af69b0bec7f5b73ad5edad581742cc4e6b10a4f88a3d1db48e97e3129df02f3e (also in
  data/avia/gravd/inputs.lock; check with: cd data/avia/gravd && sha256sum -c inputs.lock)
In the public repository: yes, beside this description.
Obtained: downloaded as published (fog fetch or by hand).
From: NOAA NGS GRAV-D, Block EN02 release BETA2.
URL: https://geodesy.noaa.gov/pub/grav-d/EN02/NGS_GRAVD_Block_EN02_BETA2.zip
Check it yourself: fog verify avia-gravd (tools/) downloads it again into a temporary directory and
  compares it with this hash.
Collected: 2026-09-29
Licence: public-domain
Sources:
- NOAA National Geodetic Survey, GRAV-D (Gravity for the Redefinition of the American Vertical
  Datum), Block EN02 release BETA2 <https://geodesy.noaa.gov/GRAV-D/>
Evidence class (rule 10): check
Where the Earth's radius enters:
- g: NOAA removed the Eötvös effect with the GRS80 radii; putting it back needs those radii
- ve, vn: velocities from the published positions on the GRS80 ellipsoid (computed by fog)
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Uncorrected airborne gravity that agrees between
  eastbound and westbound lines, or a best fraction of the globe's Eötvös correction of 0 rather
  than 1.
Built by: fog build avia-gravd
Generated files:
- src/data/presets/avia-gravd.js
Code:
- tools/internal/build/aviagravd
- src/tabs/avia/eot.js
Shown on the page:
- #tab-avia/avia-h2eo
Notes: One block = one input zip; adding a block is one more input line with its URL, then fog
  fetch, fog lock, fog build. Only the main data file of each zip is read (not the supplement: cross
  lines and transits).

Written by `fog build explain` from data/avia/gravd/dataset.json and data/avia/gravd/inputs.lock: edit those, not this file.
